WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.483 --> 00:00:01.316
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.316 --> 00:00:02.875
Welcome to our Homeroom livestream,

00:00:02.875 --> 00:00:04.215
Sal here from Khan Academy,

00:00:04.215 --> 00:00:06.915
really excited about the
conversation we're about to have

00:00:06.915 --> 00:00:09.505
with David Banks, who is really
one of the leading educators

00:00:09.505 --> 00:00:12.685
in the country, president of
the Eagle Academy Foundation.

00:00:12.685 --> 00:00:13.765
But before we get into that,

00:00:13.765 --> 00:00:15.585
I will make my standard announcements,

00:00:15.585 --> 00:00:18.215
a reminder to everyone
that Khan Academy is

00:00:18.215 --> 00:00:19.645
a not-for-profit organization,

00:00:19.645 --> 00:00:21.875
we can only exist through
philanthropic donations.

00:00:21.875 --> 00:00:23.505
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:23.505 --> 00:00:25.955
please think about going
to Khanacademy.org/donate

00:00:25.955 --> 00:00:28.835
and donations of all
size make a difference.

00:00:28.835 --> 00:00:30.575
I also want to give a special shout out

00:00:30.575 --> 00:00:32.775
to several organizations
that have helped us

00:00:32.775 --> 00:00:35.225
over the last few months, really.

00:00:35.225 --> 00:00:37.775
Some of these well
before that, many years,

00:00:37.775 --> 00:00:39.075
long time supporters.

00:00:39.075 --> 00:00:41.995
But when they saw that we were
already running at a deficit

00:00:41.995 --> 00:00:44.135
pre-COVID, and that deficit only increased

00:00:44.135 --> 00:00:46.225
'cause our costs increase
'cause obviously,

00:00:46.225 --> 00:00:49.415
our server costs have gone
up, two to three X of normal,

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:50.795
we're trying to accelerate a whole series

00:00:50.795 --> 00:00:52.455
of programs and content.

00:00:52.455 --> 00:00:55.205
So special thanks to Bank of
America, Google.org, AT&amp;T,

00:00:55.205 --> 00:00:57.655
fastly, Novartis, and many others,

00:00:57.655 --> 00:00:58.885
in helping us close this gap,

00:00:58.885 --> 00:01:01.095
as we are trying to step
up in any way we can

00:01:01.095 --> 00:01:03.505
to get the country and actually the world

00:01:03.505 --> 00:01:06.735
through distance learning and
actually get to a place where

00:01:06.735 --> 00:01:09.615
a personalized education
and learning not being bound

00:01:09.615 --> 00:01:13.295
by time or space becomes a
norm and not an exception.

00:01:13.295 --> 00:01:16.655
So with that, I'm really
excited to introduce David.

00:01:16.655 --> 00:01:19.045
Hi David, good to see you.

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:21.858
President and CEO of
Eagle Academy Foundation.

00:01:23.115 --> 00:01:26.068
- Good to see you as
well, Sal, appreciate it.

00:01:27.755 --> 00:01:30.095
- So David, maybe a good place to start is

00:01:30.095 --> 00:01:33.855
tell us what is the Eagle Academy?

00:01:33.855 --> 00:01:37.698
And why have you put so
much energy into this?

00:01:39.035 --> 00:01:43.195
- So the Eagle Academy
is a network of all boys

00:01:43.195 --> 00:01:45.595
public schools in New York City,

00:01:45.595 --> 00:01:47.435
where we work with young men of color

00:01:47.435 --> 00:01:49.518
from grade six to grade 12.

00:01:50.505 --> 00:01:53.345
We've got a school in every
borough in New York City,

00:01:53.345 --> 00:01:55.775
as well as one in Newark, New Jersey.

00:01:55.775 --> 00:01:58.365
So as we sit here today,
we got 3,000 young men

00:01:58.365 --> 00:02:02.635
across our schools, and have
graduated about 2,000 young men

00:02:02.635 --> 00:02:04.565
over the years and sent them onto colleges

00:02:04.565 --> 00:02:06.545
and universities across the country.

00:02:06.545 --> 00:02:09.125
We started in collaboration
with the 100 Black Men

00:02:09.125 --> 00:02:11.645
in New York, really as a response

00:02:11.645 --> 00:02:13.795
to the national crisis that we have seen,

00:02:13.795 --> 00:02:15.595
so many of our young men have faced,

00:02:15.595 --> 00:02:17.645
where they've not
graduated from high school

00:02:17.645 --> 00:02:21.285
and not presenting themselves
for opportunities in industry.

00:02:21.285 --> 00:02:24.235
Getting caught up in getting incarcerated,

00:02:24.235 --> 00:02:26.265
caught up in the criminal justice system.

00:02:26.265 --> 00:02:29.256
All the things that we hear
about on a regular basis,

00:02:29.256 --> 00:02:32.085
but there's really been relatively

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:34.915
little response to that crisis.

00:02:34.915 --> 00:02:37.825
And so, Eagle Academy was
created really in many ways

00:02:37.825 --> 00:02:40.365
as a response to give young men a place

00:02:40.365 --> 00:02:41.885
where they can hope and dream,

00:02:41.885 --> 00:02:44.288
and reach their full potential.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.845
- And there's at least two
really interesting things

00:02:47.845 --> 00:02:49.345
that might be surprising for some folks,

00:02:49.345 --> 00:02:50.715
that you just mentioned.

00:02:50.715 --> 00:02:53.425
This isn't your
traditional charter school,

00:02:53.425 --> 00:02:55.715
these are actual public
schools in the New York City

00:02:55.715 --> 00:02:58.835
public school system, I guess
in Newark in one case as well.

00:02:58.835 --> 00:03:00.085
And then the other surprising thing is

00:03:00.085 --> 00:03:03.755
you don't hear a lot about
single sex schools anymore.

00:03:03.755 --> 00:03:07.578
This is a school for young
men, at risk young men.

00:03:08.655 --> 00:03:11.455
First of all, how does
the first thing work?

00:03:11.455 --> 00:03:13.575
How does a branded system of schools

00:03:13.575 --> 00:03:15.485
within the public school system work,

00:03:15.485 --> 00:03:16.685
and why did you make that decision?

00:03:16.685 --> 00:03:20.705
And then why the decision
to focus only on young men?

00:03:20.705 --> 00:03:24.425
- Well many years ago, when
Mike Bloomberg was the Mayor

00:03:24.425 --> 00:03:27.135
of New York City, he cast a wide net

00:03:27.135 --> 00:03:29.685
in terms of trying to
get other not-for-profits

00:03:29.685 --> 00:03:31.505
and organizations to get more involved

00:03:31.505 --> 00:03:33.895
in the public education system.

00:03:33.895 --> 00:03:38.895
And in doing so, we became
part of the 21st Century

00:03:40.365 --> 00:03:42.875
Education Reform Effort that
was happening in New York,

00:03:42.875 --> 00:03:45.415
sponsored by the Gates Foundation.

00:03:45.415 --> 00:03:47.675
Where they said, listen,
this is a huge system

00:03:47.675 --> 00:03:49.792
and we need others to get involved.

00:03:49.792 --> 00:03:52.585
And so that's when our
organization, 100 Black Men,

00:03:52.585 --> 00:03:53.455
decided to get involved.

00:03:53.455 --> 00:03:56.355
Over the years, it had
become politically incorrect

00:03:56.355 --> 00:03:58.158
to have single gender schools.

00:03:59.235 --> 00:04:02.205
But we leaned into that
space really to say,

00:04:02.205 --> 00:04:06.185
what is it that causes young
men to believe in themselves

00:04:06.185 --> 00:04:08.995
and to focus on their own potential?

00:04:08.995 --> 00:04:10.595
And I was the principal of the school.

00:04:10.595 --> 00:04:14.115
We created it in the Bronx with
a group of ninth grade boys,

00:04:14.115 --> 00:04:16.415
and quickly realized that if
we were going to do this well,

00:04:16.415 --> 00:04:18.755
we needed to start earlier
than the ninth grade.

00:04:18.755 --> 00:04:20.235
And our model soon after,

00:04:20.235 --> 00:04:22.515
became a grade six through 12 model.

00:04:22.515 --> 00:04:25.995
But we were intentional in
saying that we wanted it

00:04:25.995 --> 00:04:27.945
to be traditional public schools,

00:04:27.945 --> 00:04:30.795
because philosophically,
we really felt as though

00:04:30.795 --> 00:04:34.075
you can't charter your way
out of this education morass,

00:04:34.075 --> 00:04:38.215
charters oughta be a big part
of the response in education,

00:04:38.215 --> 00:04:40.615
but over 90% of high
school kids in America

00:04:40.615 --> 00:04:42.995
still want a regular traditional schools

00:04:42.995 --> 00:04:44.355
like most of us went to.

00:04:44.355 --> 00:04:46.515
And if you can't move
the needle in that space,

00:04:46.515 --> 00:04:48.225
you're just playing around on the margins.

00:04:48.225 --> 00:04:50.575
And so we wanted to be able
to lean into that space

00:04:50.575 --> 00:04:51.925
and that's what we've done.

00:04:52.985 --> 00:04:54.995
- And what does it look like?

00:04:54.995 --> 00:04:58.005
What's different about an
Eagle Academy public school,

00:04:58.005 --> 00:04:59.978
other than the single gender focus?

00:05:00.935 --> 00:05:02.845
What are y'all doing that
you think is different?

00:05:02.845 --> 00:05:03.678
And what have you learned

00:05:03.678 --> 00:05:05.735
that has been particularly effective?

00:05:05.735 --> 00:05:07.055
- Several things.

00:05:07.055 --> 00:05:09.375
It's about the culture that you establish

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:12.415
that says to young men that
we deeply believe in you.

00:05:12.415 --> 00:05:14.888
And that may seem like
something really simple,

00:05:15.765 --> 00:05:17.225
but it is not.

00:05:17.225 --> 00:05:20.635
You have to remember that 80%
of teachers across America

00:05:20.635 --> 00:05:25.025
are white and female, and
don't necessarily have

00:05:25.025 --> 00:05:29.330
a background or training
in how to work with boys.

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:31.105
First of all, there's some
significant differences

00:05:31.105 --> 00:05:34.175
between the energy that boys
can bring to the classroom

00:05:34.175 --> 00:05:37.735
and that girls can bring
to the classroom, broadly.

00:05:37.735 --> 00:05:41.575
But when you start talking
about boys of color,

00:05:41.575 --> 00:05:46.015
there are some unique
challenges that those young men

00:05:46.015 --> 00:05:48.105
bring to the classroom everyday.

00:05:48.105 --> 00:05:50.215
And there's a unique
energy that they bring.

00:05:50.215 --> 00:05:54.465
And very often, the boys find themselves

00:05:54.465 --> 00:05:58.325
getting disciplined in school,
being called out for things

00:05:59.215 --> 00:06:00.763
that are really not bad behavior,

00:06:00.763 --> 00:06:05.135
it's just having teachers
having a level of sensitivity

00:06:05.135 --> 00:06:07.415
and understanding who the boys are,

00:06:07.415 --> 00:06:08.895
what their backgrounds are,

00:06:08.895 --> 00:06:13.325
and recognizing that there's
a certain way, as adults,

00:06:13.325 --> 00:06:15.395
that we have to show up if we wanna get

00:06:15.395 --> 00:06:16.855
the most out of these young men.

00:06:16.855 --> 00:06:19.895
So we engage them in all kinds
of professional development

00:06:19.895 --> 00:06:21.235
and training for our teachers

00:06:21.235 --> 00:06:24.465
around what does it mean to
meet the boys where they are?

00:06:24.465 --> 00:06:28.195
We've got expanded day programming.

00:06:28.195 --> 00:06:29.508
That really is about what are the things

00:06:29.508 --> 00:06:31.428
that boys are really interested in?

00:06:32.475 --> 00:06:33.715
We've got game rooms.

00:06:33.715 --> 00:06:34.548
You think about it.

00:06:34.548 --> 00:06:39.182
The boys love to play video
games, but yet in schools,

00:06:40.325 --> 00:06:43.845
we don't often utilize the kinds of things

00:06:43.845 --> 00:06:45.125
that boys are interested in.

00:06:45.125 --> 00:06:48.325
We use a one size fits
all kind of approach.

00:06:48.325 --> 00:06:50.385
Boys love to compete,

00:06:50.385 --> 00:06:52.235
but they don't like to compete one-on-one.

00:06:52.235 --> 00:06:54.315
They wanna compete my
group against your group.

00:06:54.315 --> 00:06:58.490
So how do you frame a
spirit of competition

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:03.490
into how you do your day-to-day
interactions in school?

00:07:03.525 --> 00:07:05.295
But one of the most
important things that we do,

00:07:05.295 --> 00:07:08.955
it's just about the culture
of saying to young men,

00:07:08.955 --> 00:07:12.345
we see you, we recognize
all of the challenges

00:07:12.345 --> 00:07:13.965
that you face on a daily basis.

00:07:13.965 --> 00:07:16.435
We have a daily town hall
meeting for half an hour

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:18.835
before the young man stepped
foot into a classroom.

00:07:18.835 --> 00:07:22.965
We pour libation and they get
a chance just to reflect on

00:07:22.965 --> 00:07:24.575
what's happening in their lives,

00:07:24.575 --> 00:07:26.645
and the trauma that so
many of them are feeling

00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:28.051
each and every day.

00:07:28.051 --> 00:07:30.045
That's not the kind of
thing that you can measure

00:07:30.045 --> 00:07:31.865
in a standardized test,

00:07:31.865 --> 00:07:34.765
but it's the kind of thing
that says to the young men,

00:07:34.765 --> 00:07:37.825
we see you and we're here for you.

00:07:37.825 --> 00:07:40.028
And it makes all the
difference in the world.

00:07:41.045 --> 00:07:42.555
- I can imagine that's very powerful.

00:07:42.555 --> 00:07:45.915
And I wanna encourage everyone
watching to put questions

00:07:45.915 --> 00:07:47.615
on Facebook and YouTube

00:07:47.615 --> 00:07:49.912
about really anything regarding education.

00:07:49.912 --> 00:07:52.245
And it could be a single gender education,

00:07:52.245 --> 00:07:55.555
it could be this question
around public schools,

00:07:55.555 --> 00:07:56.388
charter schools,

00:07:56.388 --> 00:07:58.095
it could be how we're
gonna do distance learning,

00:07:58.095 --> 00:08:00.045
and we'll talk a little
bit more about that.

00:08:00.045 --> 00:08:01.655
But David, as you've gone on this journey,

00:08:01.655 --> 00:08:02.575
I guess how many, first of all,

00:08:02.575 --> 00:08:04.065
how many years has it been?

00:08:04.065 --> 00:08:06.945
And what has been kind of
your, maybe some of your things

00:08:06.945 --> 00:08:08.305
you've been struggling the most with

00:08:08.305 --> 00:08:09.705
that you're still trying to figure out?

00:08:09.705 --> 00:08:11.805
And then what have been
some of the big wins

00:08:11.805 --> 00:08:13.765
that you're like, wow
that's a powerful data point

00:08:13.765 --> 00:08:15.975
that shows that we're on the right track?

00:08:15.975 --> 00:08:18.515
- Well, there are a few things,
Sal, I would tell you that.

00:08:18.515 --> 00:08:20.515
First of all, I've been in
education for over 30 years

00:08:20.515 --> 00:08:23.795
myself, having been a teacher,
an assistant principal,

00:08:23.795 --> 00:08:26.275
founding principal, not
only of the Eagle Academy,

00:08:26.275 --> 00:08:28.965
but have a wonderful coed
school in the Bronx, as well.

00:08:28.965 --> 00:08:31.465
So this is a space that
I've dedicated my life to.

00:08:32.335 --> 00:08:34.445
There are a number of
things that we have learned

00:08:34.445 --> 00:08:36.645
in the space for boys, but we've also seen

00:08:36.645 --> 00:08:40.058
very broadly really
for boys and for girls.

00:08:41.875 --> 00:08:43.715
Internationally, there've
been some studies

00:08:43.715 --> 00:08:45.331
that have been done around boys.

00:08:45.331 --> 00:08:47.735
And the biggest thing that emerges is that

00:08:47.735 --> 00:08:50.748
boys disconnect from school
because school is boring.

00:08:51.735 --> 00:08:55.085
The connection to what
they're doing in class

00:08:55.085 --> 00:08:56.865
to what their real interests are,

00:08:56.865 --> 00:08:58.835
very often are not aligned.

00:08:58.835 --> 00:09:01.635
And most teachers will tell
you that, they recognize that.

00:09:01.635 --> 00:09:03.705
But they struggle with how to figure out

00:09:03.705 --> 00:09:06.325
how to make that connection.

00:09:06.325 --> 00:09:09.858
So intuitively they know
that boys checkout easily.

00:09:10.855 --> 00:09:13.245
But you gotta figure out
how do you make your lessons

00:09:13.245 --> 00:09:15.935
more fast paced, more engaging,

00:09:15.935 --> 00:09:18.125
in order for boys to really stay focused.

00:09:18.125 --> 00:09:19.835
And they've gotta be relevant.

00:09:19.835 --> 00:09:21.245
You've gotta be talking about issues

00:09:21.245 --> 00:09:22.655
that really do make a difference.

00:09:22.655 --> 00:09:24.775
And that's where I think
we begin to see the overlap

00:09:24.775 --> 00:09:26.835
between boys and girls.

00:09:26.835 --> 00:09:27.855
I'm a firm believer,

00:09:27.855 --> 00:09:30.955
after having spent three
decades in this space,

00:09:30.955 --> 00:09:35.625
that we are doing education the wrong way

00:09:35.625 --> 00:09:37.945
if we really want everybody to win.

00:09:37.945 --> 00:09:39.815
And what I mean by that, Sal, is that

00:09:40.747 --> 00:09:43.995
we've got to reimagine,
we've got to recreate

00:09:43.995 --> 00:09:47.945
how we are doing the education experience.

00:09:47.945 --> 00:09:51.115
And so what I mean by
that is that you've got,

00:09:51.115 --> 00:09:53.105
I think we do a really
good job of schooling,

00:09:53.105 --> 00:09:55.245
but not necessarily such
a great job of educating.

00:09:55.245 --> 00:09:56.925
And there's a difference, right?

00:09:56.925 --> 00:09:59.875
There's the routine of
going to school everyday.

00:09:59.875 --> 00:10:02.415
But when you think about the
fact that in New York City,

00:10:02.415 --> 00:10:04.365
if somebody sends me their child at pre-K

00:10:04.365 --> 00:10:05.805
and I have them all the
way through 12th grade,

00:10:05.805 --> 00:10:08.265
that means I've had them for 14 years.

00:10:08.265 --> 00:10:10.285
And in New York City, if you
had a child for 14 years,

00:10:10.285 --> 00:10:13.861
we taxpayers have spent over $300,000

00:10:13.861 --> 00:10:16.195
on each one of those children.

00:10:16.195 --> 00:10:18.745
So you kind of say, well what's
our return on investment?

00:10:18.745 --> 00:10:20.565
You got kids who come
out after 12th grade,

00:10:20.565 --> 00:10:21.965
they can't tell you the difference between

00:10:21.965 --> 00:10:24.415
a Congressman and a Councilman.

00:10:24.415 --> 00:10:26.455
They don't know, if they
wanted to start a business,

00:10:26.455 --> 00:10:27.765
where to even begin.

00:10:27.765 --> 00:10:31.565
There're just too many things
that we are not aware of.

00:10:31.565 --> 00:10:33.485
And one of the things
I'm deeply troubled by

00:10:33.485 --> 00:10:36.705
is the fact that you look
at a presidential election,

00:10:36.705 --> 00:10:40.445
only 56% of age eligible Americans

00:10:40.445 --> 00:10:42.665
even vote in a presidential election.

00:10:42.665 --> 00:10:45.115
That's a little more than
half of our population.

00:10:45.115 --> 00:10:45.948
Why is that?

00:10:45.948 --> 00:10:47.455
Why are people so apathetic?

00:10:47.455 --> 00:10:51.675
I think that is because we
don't prepare young people

00:10:51.675 --> 00:10:55.845
through that K-12 experience
to be engaged citizens.

00:10:55.845 --> 00:10:59.405
We hang that slogan up in
every school in America,

00:10:59.405 --> 00:11:01.585
but that's not actually what we practice.

00:11:01.585 --> 00:11:05.575
We practice kids getting ready
to take standardized exams,

00:11:05.575 --> 00:11:08.255
that don't necessarily
have any real relevance

00:11:08.255 --> 00:11:10.455
to what their lives
really should be about.

00:11:10.455 --> 00:11:12.905
And that's what I have seen
in that space with boys,

00:11:12.905 --> 00:11:15.675
I've seen it in that
space with boys and girls.

00:11:15.675 --> 00:11:18.415
If we don't figure out ways
to make their education

00:11:18.415 --> 00:11:21.288
much more relevant to
their day-to-day lives,

00:11:22.125 --> 00:11:24.045
we will just be doing the routine

00:11:24.045 --> 00:11:25.795
of going to school everyday,

00:11:25.795 --> 00:11:28.205
but not necessarily preparing young people

00:11:28.205 --> 00:11:30.605
what they need to be
successful in their future.

00:11:31.475 --> 00:11:34.335
- And so, as you engage, arguably a lot

00:11:34.335 --> 00:11:36.075
of what you're saying
applies to both genders,

00:11:36.075 --> 00:11:38.925
but as you engage in this way,

00:11:38.925 --> 00:11:40.717
what are you seeing of your graduates?

00:11:40.717 --> 00:11:43.565
Are they being more successful in college?

00:11:43.565 --> 00:11:46.675
Or are they just more
confident in their lives?

00:11:46.675 --> 00:11:49.025
What data points have
you have you seen so far?

00:11:50.055 --> 00:11:52.145
- What we have seen is
something that sometimes

00:11:52.145 --> 00:11:53.515
is difficult to measure.

00:11:53.515 --> 00:11:58.015
And so, how do you measure grit?

00:11:58.015 --> 00:12:00.085
How do you measure determination?

00:12:00.085 --> 00:12:01.258
That's something that people have really

00:12:01.258 --> 00:12:02.628
been struggling with.

00:12:03.935 --> 00:12:07.245
Everyday at Eagle Academy, our young men

00:12:07.245 --> 00:12:08.902
recite the poem, "Invictus."

00:12:09.795 --> 00:12:12.105
And "Invictus" is the poem of resilience.

00:12:12.105 --> 00:12:14.675
It was the poem recited
by Nelson Mandela everyday

00:12:14.675 --> 00:12:17.585
when he was locked up on
Robben Island for 27 years.

00:12:17.585 --> 00:12:19.235
It was the favorite poem of John Lewis,

00:12:19.235 --> 00:12:21.405
who just recently passed away.

00:12:21.405 --> 00:12:23.055
It's a poem that really talks about

00:12:23.055 --> 00:12:25.035
when you get knocked
down, how to get back up.

00:12:25.035 --> 00:12:27.925
Well we talk about that
poem, and we recite that poem

00:12:27.925 --> 00:12:32.138
across with all 3,000 of our
young men each and every day.

00:12:32.138 --> 00:12:36.285
And that poem really
represents what we want

00:12:36.285 --> 00:12:39.115
from our young men, to know
that life is going to hit you,

00:12:39.115 --> 00:12:40.635
and you're gonna get knocked down.

00:12:40.635 --> 00:12:42.455
But our young men who have
gone, who've graduated

00:12:42.455 --> 00:12:44.115
and gone off to colleges and universities

00:12:44.115 --> 00:12:46.895
across the country, have
been able to persevere.

00:12:46.895 --> 00:12:48.895
We've been able to get our
numbers, graduation numbers

00:12:48.895 --> 00:12:51.135
higher than the national average.

00:12:51.135 --> 00:12:53.315
And we believe that's
because our young men

00:12:53.315 --> 00:12:55.555
may not come out with
the highest test scores,

00:12:55.555 --> 00:12:57.855
but they come out with
something inside of them,

00:12:57.855 --> 00:13:00.345
which talks about how to stay focused,

00:13:00.345 --> 00:13:01.845
that when the going gets tough,

00:13:01.845 --> 00:13:04.845
you've got to continue
to fight and to press on.

00:13:04.845 --> 00:13:09.845
And that your destiny is
wonderful and full of potential.

00:13:10.955 --> 00:13:13.034
And that is what the
whole Eagle experience

00:13:13.034 --> 00:13:15.505
has really represented for them.

00:13:15.505 --> 00:13:17.534
And that's what we
continue to push forward.

00:13:17.534 --> 00:13:19.855
Listen, we're still very
much a work in progress.

00:13:19.855 --> 00:13:21.245
We're still learning everyday.

00:13:21.245 --> 00:13:24.045
We're very much a learning organization.

00:13:24.045 --> 00:13:27.488
But our young men have been
doing well, and doing us proud.

00:13:28.955 --> 00:13:30.625
- Yeah, no, I could imagine.

00:13:30.625 --> 00:13:33.465
And a lot of great questions
coming in from social media,

00:13:33.465 --> 00:13:37.515
from Facebook (mumbles) is asking,

00:13:37.515 --> 00:13:39.775
are there strategies
already in place to educate

00:13:39.775 --> 00:13:42.185
the parents simultaneously,
to engage them,

00:13:42.185 --> 00:13:44.355
to nurture their kids, and
the importance of their role

00:13:44.355 --> 00:13:47.075
in their kid's life while
educating and nurturing the kids?

00:13:47.075 --> 00:13:48.815
I believe that parents
and teachers equally

00:13:48.815 --> 00:13:50.965
play an important role in
how we shape their thoughts,

00:13:50.965 --> 00:13:52.705
actions, and upbringing.

00:13:52.705 --> 00:13:53.548
Thoughts there?

00:13:54.805 --> 00:13:56.022
- Huge part of the work
that we do at Eagle.

00:13:56.022 --> 00:13:58.575
One of the reasons why
we've been as successful

00:13:58.575 --> 00:14:02.575
as we have been, is because
we don't just talk about

00:14:02.575 --> 00:14:05.525
parents being involved and
engaged, we practice that.

00:14:05.525 --> 00:14:08.675
We let our parents know
we are partners in this.

00:14:08.675 --> 00:14:10.995
So let me give you one example
of something that we do

00:14:10.995 --> 00:14:13.235
that most schools across America don't do.

00:14:13.235 --> 00:14:16.655
We gather our parents
for community gatherings,

00:14:16.655 --> 00:14:19.468
with their teachers and
principals on Saturday mornings.

00:14:20.585 --> 00:14:22.125
Now why is that important?

00:14:22.125 --> 00:14:24.915
Because the traditional
Wednesday, Thursday night

00:14:24.915 --> 00:14:28.155
after work meeting is something
that people have been doing

00:14:28.155 --> 00:14:29.435
all across America.

00:14:29.435 --> 00:14:32.165
We lament the fact that lots
of parents don't come out

00:14:32.165 --> 00:14:33.245
and don't get involved.

00:14:33.245 --> 00:14:34.385
Well, they've got other kids,

00:14:34.385 --> 00:14:37.445
they've got two or three
jobs, it's really tough.

00:14:37.445 --> 00:14:40.015
And that's because we
generally set our schedule

00:14:40.015 --> 00:14:42.205
of how we want parents to be involved.

00:14:42.205 --> 00:14:47.035
We set that in a space that
benefits school personnel.

00:14:47.035 --> 00:14:48.685
It's good for us to show
up on a Wednesday night,

00:14:48.685 --> 00:14:49.805
we're already in school,

00:14:49.805 --> 00:14:52.105
we just stick around for
a couple of extra hours.

00:14:52.105 --> 00:14:54.855
But we had an idea that if we met parents

00:14:54.855 --> 00:14:57.145
on Saturday mornings, we could
get them much more involved.

00:14:57.145 --> 00:14:59.125
And it has been absolutely amazing.

00:14:59.125 --> 00:15:01.895
The response that we've
gotten from our parents

00:15:01.895 --> 00:15:03.465
when they show up on Saturdays,

00:15:03.465 --> 00:15:06.605
we've encouraged them to take the lead.

00:15:06.605 --> 00:15:08.585
And so it's not just the school principal

00:15:08.585 --> 00:15:10.145
who has to run those meetings,

00:15:10.145 --> 00:15:12.612
but the parents themselves
are leading the work

00:15:12.612 --> 00:15:13.765
and the efforts.

00:15:13.765 --> 00:15:15.565
And how you engage the community,

00:15:15.565 --> 00:15:18.125
it's a huge part of the DNA of what we do.

00:15:18.125 --> 00:15:21.575
And a big part of reason
why we've been so successful

00:15:21.575 --> 00:15:24.195
with our young men, because their parents

00:15:24.195 --> 00:15:26.645
have been running this
race side by side with us.

00:15:28.081 --> 00:15:28.914
- I love that idea.

00:15:28.914 --> 00:15:30.415
A lot of the things
we've already mentioned,

00:15:30.415 --> 00:15:33.605
feel like things that many
schools can replicate.

00:15:33.605 --> 00:15:36.155
These aren't like completely
re-engineering everything.

00:15:36.155 --> 00:15:38.245
Making sure that kids feel supported,

00:15:38.245 --> 00:15:39.195
believe in themselves.

00:15:39.195 --> 00:15:40.365
I love "Invictus."

00:15:40.365 --> 00:15:43.065
So I love the fact that
y'all are reciting that,

00:15:43.065 --> 00:15:44.565
or invoking that everyday.

00:15:44.565 --> 00:15:46.625
Having Saturday meetings,

00:15:46.625 --> 00:15:49.075
so it's much easier for parents to engage.

00:15:49.075 --> 00:15:50.525
A lot of questions coming in.

00:15:51.765 --> 00:15:55.335
From YouTube, Smart Bear asks,

00:15:55.335 --> 00:15:58.315
David, I guess you already
talked a little bit about this,

00:15:58.315 --> 00:15:59.535
why you started Eagle Academy,

00:15:59.535 --> 00:16:02.535
but what are your inspirations?

00:16:02.535 --> 00:16:04.285
Maybe I'll broaden the question.

00:16:04.285 --> 00:16:05.435
Describe this path.

00:16:05.435 --> 00:16:07.715
You've talked about spending
three decades in education.

00:16:07.715 --> 00:16:09.135
Why did you pick,

00:16:09.135 --> 00:16:12.075
what were you like in high
school and middle school?

00:16:12.075 --> 00:16:13.675
What brought you down this path?

00:16:13.675 --> 00:16:15.425
And at the end of your career,

00:16:15.425 --> 00:16:18.368
what do you hope you can look
back on and say happened?

00:16:19.285 --> 00:16:22.085
- I didn't go to school
initially to become an educator.

00:16:23.145 --> 00:16:27.035
I started out as an engineering
major at Rutgers University.

00:16:27.035 --> 00:16:29.205
And after a couple of years, I
switched to political science

00:16:29.205 --> 00:16:30.875
with the intention of going to law school,

00:16:30.875 --> 00:16:32.245
which I ultimately did.

00:16:32.245 --> 00:16:34.442
I started teaching and having a family,

00:16:34.442 --> 00:16:37.045
and then went to law school at night.

00:16:37.045 --> 00:16:40.040
Left education, never thinking
that I was coming back,

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:42.285
and worked for the New York City

00:16:42.285 --> 00:16:44.705
and New York State Department of Law.

00:16:44.705 --> 00:16:46.085
But as fate would have it,

00:16:46.085 --> 00:16:48.605
I was really supposed to be
working with young people.

00:16:48.605 --> 00:16:52.315
And I came back and became
a school administrator,

00:16:52.315 --> 00:16:54.435
and I've had a wonderful career,

00:16:54.435 --> 00:16:56.658
and am absolutely living my purpose.

00:16:57.626 --> 00:16:59.075
- So let me just ask one question on that.

00:16:59.075 --> 00:17:01.215
So you were a practicing lawyer?

00:17:01.215 --> 00:17:03.355
And then you somehow went into become,

00:17:03.355 --> 00:17:04.555
I mean, what was that?

00:17:04.555 --> 00:17:07.955
I famously, I was a tech
guy, hedge fund analyst,

00:17:07.955 --> 00:17:10.215
started tutoring cousins, Khan Academy.

00:17:10.215 --> 00:17:11.695
How did you make that transition from

00:17:11.695 --> 00:17:15.335
being a practicing lawyer to
being a school administrator?

00:17:15.335 --> 00:17:20.195
- Well, it was because I
really, as a person of faith,

00:17:20.195 --> 00:17:23.915
I just, I felt my calling was,

00:17:23.915 --> 00:17:26.345
while as a child, I thought
I always to be a lawyer,

00:17:26.345 --> 00:17:29.925
but as I got older I
realized that my real calling

00:17:29.925 --> 00:17:31.525
was working with young people.

00:17:31.525 --> 00:17:35.065
And so I decided to go
back into education.

00:17:35.065 --> 00:17:38.845
And I went back and I got my certification

00:17:38.845 --> 00:17:42.145
to be a school administrator
in one semester

00:17:42.145 --> 00:17:44.608
by going to three
colleges at the same time,

00:17:46.555 --> 00:17:49.888
which people have found
to be somewhat inspiring.

00:17:50.845 --> 00:17:53.055
But for me, it was just an example that

00:17:53.055 --> 00:17:54.595
when you're very focused on what it is

00:17:54.595 --> 00:17:56.928
that you're supposed to be doing in life,

00:17:57.785 --> 00:18:00.545
they say the universe will
conspire for your success,

00:18:00.545 --> 00:18:04.205
and doors will open if you're
prepared to put in the work.

00:18:04.205 --> 00:18:05.905
And I was prepared to put in the work.

00:18:05.905 --> 00:18:07.725
So for me on a personal level,

00:18:07.725 --> 00:18:10.725
that's kind of how I
got to be where I'm at,

00:18:10.725 --> 00:18:13.085
kind of being back in
this education space.

00:18:13.085 --> 00:18:15.515
But I was inspired around
this work of Eagle Academy

00:18:15.515 --> 00:18:19.815
because Columbia University
put out a report that said that

00:18:20.745 --> 00:18:25.585
75% of the state's prison
population was coming from

00:18:25.585 --> 00:18:27.405
seven neighborhoods in New York City.

00:18:27.405 --> 00:18:29.915
So you think about that, Sal, right?

00:18:29.915 --> 00:18:32.995
75%, if you look at the
entire state of New York,

00:18:32.995 --> 00:18:36.785
75% of the inmates were coming,
not just from New York City,

00:18:36.785 --> 00:18:40.135
but from seven very specific
zip codes in New York City,

00:18:40.135 --> 00:18:41.485
this is where people of color are.

00:18:41.485 --> 00:18:45.638
Central Harlem, the Lower
East Side, Southeast Queens,

00:18:47.925 --> 00:18:50.165
Bedford-Stuyvesant, it's the communities

00:18:50.165 --> 00:18:51.825
where black folk are.

00:18:51.825 --> 00:18:53.445
And people knew it.

00:18:53.445 --> 00:18:54.545
People were writing books about it,

00:18:54.545 --> 00:18:55.745
they were analyzing data,

00:18:55.745 --> 00:18:57.795
it was all kinds of
research and write papers.

00:18:57.795 --> 00:18:58.635
But at the end of the day,

00:18:58.635 --> 00:18:59.975
it seemed like the most you could hope for

00:18:59.975 --> 00:19:03.325
was a good afterschool
program for maybe 20 kids.

00:19:03.325 --> 00:19:05.615
And I said, no, we
gotta do more than that.

00:19:05.615 --> 00:19:08.865
That's the reason why we leaned
into creating Eagle Academy.

00:19:08.865 --> 00:19:12.535
Not that Eagle would be
the answer as a cure-all,

00:19:12.535 --> 00:19:14.785
but we certainly wanted it to
be a learning space for us,

00:19:14.785 --> 00:19:17.045
a lab, if you will, to figure out

00:19:18.175 --> 00:19:20.885
how do we get these boys
to believe in themselves?

00:19:20.885 --> 00:19:24.855
How do we get them to avoid
the lure of joining gangs

00:19:24.855 --> 00:19:26.485
and being on street corners?

00:19:26.485 --> 00:19:28.445
And even for boys that
don't get caught up in that,

00:19:28.445 --> 00:19:30.705
who just never reached their potential.

00:19:30.705 --> 00:19:34.345
How do we get mentors into
the lives of young men, right?

00:19:34.345 --> 00:19:37.475
You recognize that almost
70% of Black boys in America

00:19:37.475 --> 00:19:39.835
are growing up without a father at home.

00:19:39.835 --> 00:19:41.295
The hip hop generation, in many ways,

00:19:41.295 --> 00:19:43.315
is a fatherless generation.

00:19:43.315 --> 00:19:46.735
So boys become of age and they
don't have men to help them

00:19:46.735 --> 00:19:50.095
navigate the transition
from boyhood to manhood.

00:19:50.095 --> 00:19:51.165
And then when they get it wrong,

00:19:51.165 --> 00:19:53.245
we blame them, as a society.

00:19:53.245 --> 00:19:55.825
And so we said, we wanted
to lean into that space,

00:19:55.825 --> 00:19:58.155
to try to figure out some answers.

00:19:58.155 --> 00:20:01.205
And that's the journey
that we have been on.

00:20:01.205 --> 00:20:02.945
But what we have learned are lessons

00:20:02.945 --> 00:20:06.245
that we think are good for
boys, as well as for girls.

00:20:06.245 --> 00:20:07.645
So I'll give you an example,

00:20:08.555 --> 00:20:11.225
how can a boy dream of
becoming an investment banker,

00:20:11.225 --> 00:20:12.378
if he's never met one?

00:20:14.473 --> 00:20:18.145
You've gotta open up opportunities

00:20:18.145 --> 00:20:21.955
to activate the power of
possibility for young people.

00:20:21.955 --> 00:20:25.615
If all you see is a very
limited number of things

00:20:25.615 --> 00:20:27.495
in your own neighborhood or on your block,

00:20:27.495 --> 00:20:30.945
it's hard to even dream
of doing great things.

00:20:30.945 --> 00:20:33.435
So how do you bring an astronaut in?

00:20:33.435 --> 00:20:35.255
So folks get a chance to meet them

00:20:35.255 --> 00:20:37.445
and help them to demystify
what does it take

00:20:37.445 --> 00:20:39.795
to become an astronaut, or a scientist,

00:20:39.795 --> 00:20:43.338
or an investment banker, or
an architect, or a politician?

00:20:44.345 --> 00:20:45.985
These things don't just happen,

00:20:45.985 --> 00:20:47.805
you've gotta be exposed.

00:20:47.805 --> 00:20:49.428
And that's one of the things we recognize

00:20:49.428 --> 00:20:51.095
with so many young people,

00:20:51.095 --> 00:20:52.595
particularly young people of color,

00:20:52.595 --> 00:20:56.615
who are not getting exposed
to the possibilities.

00:20:56.615 --> 00:21:00.995
And that's where I think we
have great opportunity now,

00:21:00.995 --> 00:21:04.275
even in spite of this COVID pandemic.

00:21:04.275 --> 00:21:07.945
What it has thrust us into
is a space where technology

00:21:07.945 --> 00:21:11.115
can actually be the
space that closes the gap

00:21:11.115 --> 00:21:13.205
on so many of those opportunities.

00:21:13.205 --> 00:21:14.925
Where you can use technology,

00:21:14.925 --> 00:21:16.495
where you can show young people,

00:21:16.495 --> 00:21:20.975
what does it mean to work at
a Wall Street finance firm,

00:21:20.975 --> 00:21:22.835
or a Midtown law firm.

00:21:22.835 --> 00:21:24.445
You don't necessarily even have to always

00:21:24.445 --> 00:21:28.935
take the trip there, but you
can be exposed inside the walls

00:21:28.935 --> 00:21:31.585
and meet with those kinds
of career professionals,

00:21:31.585 --> 00:21:33.685
just like we're doing right now.

00:21:33.685 --> 00:21:36.075
And young people are ready.

00:21:36.075 --> 00:21:39.225
And I think, thank goodness,
so many of our adults,

00:21:39.225 --> 00:21:41.135
because we've been Zooming everyday

00:21:41.135 --> 00:21:44.625
have gotten much more
comfortable in this space.

00:21:44.625 --> 00:21:45.909
Think about this, as well, Sal.

00:21:45.909 --> 00:21:49.025
The implications in our classrooms.

00:21:49.025 --> 00:21:51.495
If you have a school that
doesn't have a good math teacher,

00:21:51.495 --> 00:21:54.725
now the school, the kids are
not getting good math results.

00:21:54.725 --> 00:21:57.455
Well, why should we be limited
by simply that math teacher

00:21:57.455 --> 00:21:59.445
that's right in front of us everyday?

00:21:59.445 --> 00:22:02.045
Why can't you take the best
math teacher that you can find,

00:22:02.045 --> 00:22:04.275
they may be in Detroit, Michigan,

00:22:04.275 --> 00:22:05.805
but through technology,

00:22:05.805 --> 00:22:08.255
why can't that person
teach those kids in Harlem?

00:22:09.415 --> 00:22:11.605
If you had the vision to do that,

00:22:11.605 --> 00:22:15.755
we now have the tools to close this gap

00:22:15.755 --> 00:22:18.125
in ways that we never had before.

00:22:18.125 --> 00:22:21.925
And that's what I mean when I
say thinking out of the box,

00:22:21.925 --> 00:22:25.108
being much more imaginative
around the possibilities

00:22:25.108 --> 00:22:28.275
of what we can do through
the use of technology,

00:22:28.275 --> 00:22:31.225
it can help to transform the
lives of so many young people.

00:22:32.675 --> 00:22:34.815
- And that kind of makes
me think about obviously,

00:22:34.815 --> 00:22:37.045
the circumstance we're in
with distance learning.

00:22:37.045 --> 00:22:41.208
I am curious, obviously
everyone's been talking about

00:22:41.208 --> 00:22:44.445
the pros and cons, mainly
cons of distance learning.

00:22:44.445 --> 00:22:46.375
But you're also saying that
there are some silver linings

00:22:46.375 --> 00:22:47.325
and things like this.

00:22:47.325 --> 00:22:48.955
How are y'all navigating it?

00:22:48.955 --> 00:22:53.145
And what do you hope is lasting,
coming out of this crisis?

00:22:53.145 --> 00:22:56.215
- We've got something that
we call a virtual village,

00:22:56.215 --> 00:22:58.245
where we have engaged
with our young people

00:22:58.245 --> 00:23:01.095
on a regular basis, even
throughout this pandemic.

00:23:01.095 --> 00:23:02.485
We're getting ready to roll out

00:23:02.485 --> 00:23:04.745
something that we're
calling a masterclass,

00:23:04.745 --> 00:23:09.745
where we're gonna bring in
experts from all kinds of areas,

00:23:09.795 --> 00:23:12.135
but all of our young people
will have an opportunity

00:23:12.135 --> 00:23:14.975
to be exposed to, that
they would not normally

00:23:14.975 --> 00:23:18.935
get that exposure in their
day-to-day schooling experience.

00:23:18.935 --> 00:23:19.895
So think about it.

00:23:19.895 --> 00:23:21.655
So many young people are
interested in sports,

00:23:21.655 --> 00:23:22.885
they're interested in music,

00:23:22.885 --> 00:23:24.865
they're interested in the music industry.

00:23:24.865 --> 00:23:27.455
But you get very little exposure to that,

00:23:27.455 --> 00:23:30.165
throughout the normal
course of your school day.

00:23:30.165 --> 00:23:33.165
But what we're doing through
this masterclass series is

00:23:33.165 --> 00:23:36.795
bringing in people who are
well-versed in all those areas,

00:23:36.795 --> 00:23:39.395
who can come in, and now just
share with these young people,

00:23:39.395 --> 00:23:40.905
all of our young people.

00:23:40.905 --> 00:23:43.552
If you're interested in getting
into the music industry,

00:23:43.552 --> 00:23:45.925
here are the things that you need to know.

00:23:45.925 --> 00:23:48.005
These are the skills
that you need to have.

00:23:48.005 --> 00:23:49.175
That's why it's gonna be important for you

00:23:49.175 --> 00:23:51.675
to continue to do your homework,

00:23:51.675 --> 00:23:54.035
and stay very focused in your
English and your math classes,

00:23:54.035 --> 00:23:56.125
because you're gonna need
these kinds of skills.

00:23:56.125 --> 00:23:58.015
What if you want to become
an investment banker?

00:23:58.015 --> 00:24:00.595
Well, first of all, what
does that even mean?

00:24:00.595 --> 00:24:02.285
And so for young people to hear from folks

00:24:02.285 --> 00:24:04.675
who can demystify that process,

00:24:04.675 --> 00:24:06.765
that's the space that
we're touching on now.

00:24:06.765 --> 00:24:09.565
But we're getting ready to
blow that out in a major way.

00:24:10.795 --> 00:24:11.725
- No, that's incredible.

00:24:11.725 --> 00:24:13.485
And that's completely gels with

00:24:13.485 --> 00:24:14.375
what a lot of we've been saying.

00:24:14.375 --> 00:24:16.075
You know, a lot of people associate school

00:24:16.075 --> 00:24:18.285
as the place to learn content standards.

00:24:18.285 --> 00:24:19.465
And it is that.

00:24:19.465 --> 00:24:21.495
But it's also the place for socialization,

00:24:21.495 --> 00:24:23.655
place to be inspired,
place to be supported.

00:24:23.655 --> 00:24:26.155
And that's not often
explicitly stated in standards.

00:24:26.155 --> 00:24:28.095
But especially during distance learning,

00:24:28.095 --> 00:24:30.565
it's a lifeline for
kids to a broader world.

00:24:30.565 --> 00:24:32.515
So that's really powerful
what you're doing.

00:24:32.515 --> 00:24:34.378
A bunch of questions here.

00:24:36.265 --> 00:24:40.215
So Smart Bear, you
answered this a little bit,

00:24:40.215 --> 00:24:41.695
why only boys,

00:24:41.695 --> 00:24:45.625
I guess Smart Bear's
question, like why not girls?

00:24:45.625 --> 00:24:46.855
Is there kind of a corollary?

00:24:46.855 --> 00:24:50.555
Are there sister schools
that are addressing that?

00:24:50.555 --> 00:24:51.855
Or just maybe the need?

00:24:51.855 --> 00:24:54.165
It sounds like you were
motivated by to some degree,

00:24:54.165 --> 00:24:58.345
this incarceration issue in
a fairly concentrated way,

00:24:58.345 --> 00:25:00.415
which is disproportionately boys.

00:25:00.415 --> 00:25:01.585
Are there other elements?

00:25:01.585 --> 00:25:03.455
Do you know folks who are,

00:25:03.455 --> 00:25:06.735
is there a benefit for
a girl only schools?

00:25:06.735 --> 00:25:08.325
Is there something there?

00:25:08.325 --> 00:25:13.305
- Absolutely, in fact, when
we opened our doors in 2004

00:25:13.305 --> 00:25:15.275
with the very first Eagle
Academy in the Bronx,

00:25:15.275 --> 00:25:17.965
and I was the principal of the school

00:25:17.965 --> 00:25:21.505
for several years earlier,
there had been schools

00:25:21.505 --> 00:25:23.575
that had already been opened up for girls.

00:25:23.575 --> 00:25:25.772
And so our sister schools in many ways,

00:25:25.772 --> 00:25:28.845
the Young Women's Leadership Academies,

00:25:28.845 --> 00:25:31.285
they opened up about four
or five years before we did.

00:25:31.285 --> 00:25:34.535
And they had already opened
up at least three schools.

00:25:34.535 --> 00:25:38.898
And in some ways we
balance the playing field.

00:25:39.865 --> 00:25:41.395
So the question was being asked of them,

00:25:41.395 --> 00:25:43.135
well hey, what about the boys?

00:25:43.135 --> 00:25:46.095
And so Eagle Academy came as a response.

00:25:46.095 --> 00:25:49.535
And so Hillary Clinton
and Claire McCaskill,

00:25:49.535 --> 00:25:51.175
and other senators at the time,

00:25:51.175 --> 00:25:53.715
put forth legislation
that essentially allowed

00:25:53.715 --> 00:25:56.785
for the proliferation of
single gender schools.

00:25:56.785 --> 00:25:59.625
But the caveat there was that
if you have a boys school,

00:25:59.625 --> 00:26:00.575
there oughta be a girls school.

00:26:00.575 --> 00:26:02.765
It doesn't have to be started
by the same organization,

00:26:02.765 --> 00:26:06.075
but the school district itself
ought to be putting in place

00:26:06.075 --> 00:26:10.235
a similarly situated academy for one,

00:26:10.235 --> 00:26:12.945
as well as providing
for the other, as well.

00:26:12.945 --> 00:26:14.265
And that's what we've been doing

00:26:14.265 --> 00:26:16.485
across wherever you see an Eagle Academy,

00:26:16.485 --> 00:26:17.895
you'll see a girls school.

00:26:17.895 --> 00:26:20.245
They're not run by our organization,

00:26:20.245 --> 00:26:23.268
but that also in place.

00:26:24.455 --> 00:26:26.979
- Yeah, no time always goes by fast

00:26:26.979 --> 00:26:28.595
when we're having an
interesting conversation.

00:26:28.595 --> 00:26:30.408
One last question is,

00:26:31.965 --> 00:26:34.165
longer term over the next few years,

00:26:34.165 --> 00:26:35.405
how do you see yourself scaling?

00:26:35.405 --> 00:26:38.315
How do you see, is the Eagle Academy a lab

00:26:38.315 --> 00:26:39.685
that other people will replicate?

00:26:39.685 --> 00:26:42.265
Is there a higher level policy changes

00:26:42.265 --> 00:26:43.695
that you think we can make to replicate

00:26:43.695 --> 00:26:44.885
some of the work you're doing?

00:26:44.885 --> 00:26:46.684
What do you think is
the five, 10 year path

00:26:46.684 --> 00:26:48.925
to getting more impact?

00:26:48.925 --> 00:26:51.325
- Yeah, so where we
are as an organization,

00:26:51.325 --> 00:26:54.105
we're not gonna be opening
up more schools per se,

00:26:54.105 --> 00:26:56.285
certainly not more Eagle Academies

00:26:56.285 --> 00:26:58.325
because we don't believe that
a brick and mortar approach

00:26:58.325 --> 00:26:59.655
is the way to go,

00:26:59.655 --> 00:27:01.845
but really looking at Eagle
in many ways as a lab.

00:27:01.845 --> 00:27:04.485
So there are school
districts that wanna open up

00:27:04.485 --> 00:27:08.505
an all boys school like
ours, not an Eagle Academy,

00:27:08.505 --> 00:27:10.285
but something similar.

00:27:10.285 --> 00:27:14.155
And we have set ourselves up
to help to support schools

00:27:14.155 --> 00:27:15.635
and districts like that,

00:27:15.635 --> 00:27:17.335
through what we call the Eagle Institute.

00:27:17.335 --> 00:27:18.445
So the Institute is set up

00:27:18.445 --> 00:27:19.915
to help to train school districts.

00:27:19.915 --> 00:27:22.025
We helped Los Angeles open the first

00:27:22.025 --> 00:27:24.445
all boys public school in their history,

00:27:24.445 --> 00:27:26.655
called the Boys Academic
Leadership Academy.

00:27:26.655 --> 00:27:28.935
It's built on the Eagle Academy model.

00:27:28.935 --> 00:27:31.115
But I will tell you Sal,
that the bigger issue

00:27:31.115 --> 00:27:34.145
is that is not single gender schools.

00:27:34.145 --> 00:27:35.255
No matter what you do, there're gonna be

00:27:35.255 --> 00:27:36.575
a limited number of single gender schools.

00:27:36.575 --> 00:27:39.055
The bigger issue is, what have we learned

00:27:39.055 --> 00:27:42.055
in working with the boys
in the single gender model,

00:27:42.055 --> 00:27:44.595
that will help teachers in coed schools

00:27:44.595 --> 00:27:47.065
who struggle everyday with the boys?

00:27:47.065 --> 00:27:49.565
And that's where our work
over the next 10 years

00:27:49.565 --> 00:27:51.175
is really gonna be focused on.

00:27:51.175 --> 00:27:53.725
We've already started that
in a number of places,

00:27:53.725 --> 00:27:56.155
where we're training the educators

00:27:56.155 --> 00:27:58.555
in these school buildings
and in these districts.

00:27:58.555 --> 00:28:01.475
And we're expanding on that
work now across the country,

00:28:01.475 --> 00:28:04.605
to help to teach them the
most promising practices

00:28:04.605 --> 00:28:06.578
around how do you get
the most out of the boys

00:28:06.578 --> 00:28:08.025
who are in your classroom?

00:28:08.025 --> 00:28:09.965
Because across America, it's the boys

00:28:09.965 --> 00:28:11.905
and it's the boys of
color, who are the ones

00:28:11.905 --> 00:28:14.525
who at the bottom of all
the indicators of success.

00:28:14.525 --> 00:28:18.085
So if you can lift them through
the work and the teachings

00:28:18.085 --> 00:28:19.455
and the things that we've learned,

00:28:19.455 --> 00:28:21.385
you will by definition,

00:28:21.385 --> 00:28:24.612
be lifting your entire school anyway.

00:28:24.612 --> 00:28:26.205
And I will just leave you with,

00:28:26.205 --> 00:28:28.745
that one of the most
important things is about

00:28:28.745 --> 00:28:32.235
how do you engage these young
men in relevant education?

00:28:32.235 --> 00:28:34.385
If the things you're doing are just about

00:28:34.385 --> 00:28:36.045
doing the routine of school,

00:28:36.045 --> 00:28:38.435
because we just supposed
to go and do our work,

00:28:38.435 --> 00:28:40.095
that's not gonna get it done.

00:28:40.095 --> 00:28:42.415
Kids have got to be engaged in things

00:28:42.415 --> 00:28:45.165
that they can see outcomes
in their own lives

00:28:45.165 --> 00:28:46.825
and in their own communities.

00:28:46.825 --> 00:28:48.575
And it can't simply just be about

00:28:48.575 --> 00:28:51.415
we're preparing kids to
take standardized exams.

00:28:51.415 --> 00:28:52.995
And then if you do standardized exams,

00:28:52.995 --> 00:28:54.525
well we say it's a great school.

00:28:54.525 --> 00:28:56.695
Kids may not know much
about anything in life,

00:28:56.695 --> 00:28:58.225
but we say it's a great school

00:28:58.225 --> 00:28:59.865
because they did well on exams.

00:28:59.865 --> 00:29:02.195
That won't get it done in working with

00:29:02.195 --> 00:29:04.465
the kind of population
that we're talking about.

00:29:04.465 --> 00:29:06.585
You've gotta talk about real world issues,

00:29:06.585 --> 00:29:09.075
and how they can wrap their
hearts and their minds,

00:29:09.075 --> 00:29:11.385
and then work around
that, and develop skills

00:29:11.385 --> 00:29:13.835
around solving problems
in their own communities.

00:29:15.005 --> 00:29:18.095
- No well, David, this
fascinating conversation,

00:29:18.095 --> 00:29:20.175
obviously we've met in
many contexts before,

00:29:20.175 --> 00:29:22.265
but I look forward to,

00:29:22.265 --> 00:29:24.185
one, seeing how we can
help on this journey,

00:29:24.185 --> 00:29:26.505
and learning from all
of the incredible work

00:29:26.505 --> 00:29:28.785
that y'all you've done
and are likely to do

00:29:28.785 --> 00:29:29.618
in the years ahead.

00:29:29.618 --> 00:29:31.277
Thank you for joining us.

00:29:31.277 --> 00:29:33.138
- Look forward to it,
thank you so much, Sal.

00:29:35.005 --> 00:29:36.195
- Well everyone, thanks again

00:29:36.195 --> 00:29:40.065
for joining our Homeroom livestream.

00:29:40.065 --> 00:29:41.645
I look forward to seeing y'all.

00:29:41.645 --> 00:29:44.835
I think we're gonna go pick up next week.

00:29:44.835 --> 00:29:48.918
But as you can tell, I'm never,
I live my life hour to hour.

00:29:50.325 --> 00:29:51.985
I need to look at the
schedules a little bit better.

00:29:51.985 --> 00:29:56.495
But anyway, stay safe and keep learning.

00:29:56.495 --> 00:29:57.675
And I'll look forward to seeing y'all

00:29:57.675 --> 00:29:58.835
on the next livestream.

00:29:58.835 --> 00:29:59.668
Thanks everyone.

